## Proof

We interpret "converges weakly to $u_0$" as weak convergence in $W^{1,p}(\Omega)$, i.e. $u_n \rightharpoonup u_0$ in $W^{1,p}(\Omega)$. By the uniform boundedness principle, $\{u_n\}$ is bounded in $W^{1,p}(\Omega)$.

The answer is governed by the **Rellich–Kondrachov compact embedding theorem**. Define the critical Sobolev exponent
$$
p^* = \begin{cases} \dfrac{dp}{d-p}, & 1 \le p < d,\\[4pt] +\infty, & p \ge d. \end{cases}
$$

**Claim.** $u_n \to u_0$ in $L^q(\Omega)$ for exactly
$$
\boxed{\;q \in [1, p^*)\;}
\qquad\text{where } p^*=\frac{dp}{d-p}\text{ if }p<d,\text{ and }q\in[1,\infty)\text{ if }p\ge d.
$$

---

### Step 1. Compact embedding range (Rellich–Kondrachov)

Since $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ is bounded with smooth boundary, the Rellich–Kondrachov theorem gives the **compact** embedding
$$
W^{1,p}(\Omega) \hookrightarrow\hookrightarrow L^q(\Omega)
$$
for precisely the following ranges of $q$:

- **Subcritical case $1 \le p < d$:** $1 \le q < p^* = \dfrac{dp}{d-p}$.
- **Critical case $p = d$:** $p^* = \infty$ formally, but the embedding into $L^\infty$ fails (only $W^{1,d}\hookrightarrow \mathrm{BMO}$); compactness holds for $1 \le q < \infty$.
- **Supercritical case $p > d$:** by Morrey's inequality $W^{1,p}(\Omega)\hookrightarrow\hookrightarrow C^{0,\alpha}(\overline\Omega)$ with $\alpha = 1-d/p$, hence compactly into $L^\infty(\Omega)$ and therefore into every $L^q(\Omega)$, $1\le q\le\infty$.

In all three cases the compact embedding into $L^q$ holds for $q \in [1, p^*)$ (with $p^*=\infty$ when $p\ge d$), which within the problem's restriction $q\in[1,\infty)$ is exactly the claimed range.

### Step 2. Weak convergence descends to $L^q$

For every $q$ in the compact range the embedding $W^{1,p}(\Omega)\hookrightarrow L^q(\Omega)$ is in particular **continuous** (linear bounded operator). A continuous linear operator is weak-to-weak continuous, so $u_n \rightharpoonup u_0$ in $W^{1,p}$ implies
$$
u_n \rightharpoonup u_0 \quad\text{in } L^q(\Omega).
$$

### Step 3. Strong convergence via the subsequence principle

Because the embedding is **compact**, every subsequence $\{u_{n_k}\}$ (still bounded in $W^{1,p}$, still weakly converging to $u_0$) admits a further subsequence $\{u_{n_{k_j}}\}$ converging **strongly** in $L^q(\Omega)$ to some limit $v$. By Step 2 this strong limit must coincide with the weak limit, so $v = u_0$.

Now apply the standard subsequence criterion: a sequence in a topological space converges to a limit iff every subsequence has a further subsequence converging to that same limit. Suppose, for contradiction, that $u_n \not\to u_0$ in $L^q$. Then there exist $\varepsilon > 0$ and a subsequence with $\|u_{n_k}-u_0\|_{L^q}\ge \varepsilon$ for all $k$. But this subsequence has a further subsequence converging strongly to $u_0$ in $L^q$ — contradiction. Hence
$$
\|u_n - u_0\|_{L^q(\Omega)} \to 0.
$$

### Step 4. Sharpness — the range cannot be extended

We show the answer is **exact**.

**When $p < d$, the endpoint $q = p^*$ fails.** Take $\Omega = B_1(0)\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ and the standard concentrating family (a fixed profile $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(B_1)$ rescaled and translated, scaled to have bounded $W^{1,p}$ norm but unbounded $L^{p^*}$ norm). Concretely, let
$$
u_n(x) = n^{(d-p)/p}\,\varphi\!\bigl(n(x-x_0)\bigr)
$$
for some $x_0\in\Omega$ with $\varphi$ supported in $B_1$, $\varphi\not\equiv 0$. Then $\|u_n\|_{L^{p^*}} = \|\varphi\|_{L^{p^*}}\cdot|\Omega|$-independent constant (in fact $\|u_n\|_{L^{p^*}}$ stays bounded but the family is **not** compact in $L^{p^*}$: translating the concentration to different points, or using the standard example of a sequence weakly converging to $0$ in $W^{1,p}$ while $\|u_n\|_{L^{p^*}}$ stays bounded away from $0$, shows non-compactness). A cleaner example: take $u_n(x) = \eta(x)\,n^{(d-p)/p}\varphi(n(x-x_0))$ with a fixed cutoff $\eta$; one checks $\|u_n\|_{W^{1,p}}\le C$ and $u_n \rightharpoonup 0$ in $W^{1,p}$ (the mass concentrates at $x_0$ and escapes weakly), yet $\|u_n\|_{L^{p^*}} \to c>0$. Thus $u_n \not\to 0$ in $L^{p^*}$. So $q=p^*$ is excluded.

**When $p \ge d$, no endpoint exists within $[1,\infty)$.** For $p=d$ the embedding into $L^\infty$ is not even continuous (Brezis–Wainger type counterexamples, or the standard logarithmic counterexample $u(x)=\log\log(1/|x|)$ near $0$ in $W^{1,d}$), so compactness into $L^\infty$ is out of the question; but the problem restricts to $q<\infty$, and for every finite $q$ compactness holds, so the full range $[1,\infty)$ is attained and is sharp within the admissible set.

### Conclusion

The sequence $\{u_n\}$ converges to $u_0$ in $L^q(\Omega)$ **if and only if**
$$
q \in \begin{cases}
\bigl[\,1,\; \dfrac{dp}{d-p}\,\bigr), & 1 \le p < d,\\[6pt]
[\,1,\; \infty), & p \ge d.
\end{cases}
$$
Equivalently, $q \in [1, p^*)$ with $p^* = \dfrac{dp}{d-p}$ the critical Sobolev exponent (and $p^*=\infty$ when $p\ge d$).

